WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.989
Consider a load followed by an independent addition.

2
00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:09.282
The load issues first, but memory may take several cycles to respond.

3
00:00:09.282 --> 00:00:18.027
If operands and resources are ready, the later addition can issue and return its result before the load completes.

4
00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:22.553
The scoreboard associates each result with its instruction.

5
00:00:22.553 --> 00:00:30.915
Retirement preserves program order, so a result becoming available is different from an instruction retiring.

6
00:00:30.915 --> 00:00:36.514
This is why ordered issue and differently ordered completion can coexist.

7
00:00:36.514 --> 00:00:42.728
Now apply the same care to performance: two issue ports describe available width.

8
00:00:42.728 --> 00:00:51.780
Dependencies, resource conflicts, branches and memory delays determine how much of that width a real workload can use.

